"Let $\nobreak\\boldsymbol{x}_1,\nobreak\\ldots,\nobreak\\boldsymbol{x}_n$ be latent points on the sphere, and let \n$$\nbe the normalized kernel operator on the sphere associated with the random geometric graph (RGG) kernel. Write\n$$\n for the trace of the cube of this operator.\n\n**Spectral detection conjecture.** The sharp threshold for distinguishing the RGG from its Erdős–Rényi counterpart is determined by\n\n$$\nn^3\\operatorname{tr}^2(\\kappa^3)=1.\n$$\n\nEquivalently, the detectable regime should satisfy $n^3\\operatorname{tr}^2(\\kappa^3)\\gg1$, while the undetectable regime should satisfy $n^3\\operatorname{tr}^2(\\kappa^3)\\ll1$.\n\nThe conjecture is motivated by both the signed triangle statistic and the Kullback–Leibler expansion, where $\\operatorname{tr}(\\kappa^3)$ arises naturally. It unifies the conjectured threshold for hard RGGs with the proved thresholds for several smooth-kernel models, but the general sharp characterization remains open."